Fix $t=2\cos(\pi k/d)$ in reduced form as above. Let
$I=\{i:d\mid\ell_i\}$ and, for $i\in I$, put
$\sigma_i=(-1)^{k\ell_i/d}$. Suppose $I\ne\varnothing$.
The eigenvalue $\lambda=2-et$ of $M_e$ has multiplicity two exactly in one
of the following cases:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $|I|=3$ and all three $\sigma_i$ agree;
\item $I=\{i,j\}$, $\sigma_i=\sigma_j=\sigma$, and, writing $c$ for the
remaining path length,
\begin{equation}
d\mid(c-1),\qquad (-1)^{k(c-1)/d}=e\sigma.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
At every other singular parameter its multiplicity is at most one.
Every eigenvalue of either matrix, singular or not, has multiplicity at most two.
